onds per year in right ascension, with the cosine-of-declination convention specified by SIMBAD, and \u22126.532 milliarcseconds per year in declination. The listed quality is A. Separately, the entry reports a radial velocity of \u221265.7 kilometers per second, with an uncertainty of 0.9 kilometers per second and an E quality flag. These are distinct catalog quantities: one describes apparent motion across the sky, while the other is the listed velocity along the line of sight.\n\nSIMBAD also records the spectral type as [WC], with a D quality flag. Its brightness entries span several bands: B 11.09, V 11.28, G 11.237219, J 8.953, H 9.076, and K 8.340. The values come from different cited records, and the entry provides uncertainties for them. Rather than one all-purpose brightness number, the catalog preserves measurements in separate bands and connects them to their sources.\n\nNGC 6543\u2019s entry is therefore more than a name attached to a point in the sky. It is a set of measurements with units, reference systems, quality labels, and citations. Read carefully, it shows both what the database records and the limits of any single number: position depends on a coordinate frame, size on the measurement wavelength, and distance on interpreting a parallax.\n\n[SIMBAD: NGC 6543](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=NGC%206543)"
